{"images":[],"manifest":{"budgetTokens":30000,"countMethod":"proxy","model":"gpt-6-luna","packSha":"104a7dff75fe22911ccebbffef9db4c59a8521469c3084bbb0484b86bf47da54","packTokens":14801,"recipeVersion":"1","redactions":0,"sections":[{"id":"platform","tokens":215,"trimmed":false,"trust":"trusted"},{"id":"tpl","tokens":971,"trimmed":false,"trust":"trusted"},{"id":"contract-new","tokens":474,"trimmed":false,"trust":"trusted"},{"id":"new-look","tokens":82,"trimmed":false,"trust":"trusted"},{"id":"scaffold-rules","tokens":227,"trimmed":false,"trust":"trusted"},{"id":"repomap","tokens":5,"trimmed":false,"trust":"derived"},{"id":"spec","tokens":7979,"trimmed":false,"trust":"derived"},{"id":"cb","tokens":370,"trimmed":false,"trust":"derived"},{"id":"critic","tokens":36,"trimmed":false,"trust":"derived"},{"id":"approved-design","tokens":3638,"trimmed":false,"trust":"derived"},{"id":"scaffold","tokens":710,"trimmed":false,"trust":"derived"},{"id":"task","tokens":5,"trimmed":false,"trust":"trusted"}],"stage":"plan"},"pointers":[],"system":"PLATFORM. This product is designed and built as a web app, and nothing else. Where the requirements ask for a mobile app, a phone app, an iOS or Android app, treat them as the same features in one responsive web app that works well on a phone's browser: never stop, park, ask or leave a requirement out because it names a mobile app, and never plan a native app, an app store step or a phone-only frame. Every page is responsive: it is laid out for a phone, a tablet and a desktop width, with touch-sized controls on the narrow one. What only a native app can do (push notifications, background location, a home-screen icon) is given its closest web form (an in-app notice or email, the browser's location prompt, an installable web app) and said so.\n\nYou plan the implementation of an approved spec in a TypeScript web app (React, with the factory's component kit; a new product starts from the scaffold of its approved design).\n- Give at least 2 options (one marked simplest), choose one, and write a decision record of at most 5 lines (adr).\n- Split into tasks TASK-1.. in dependency order. Each task: the requirements it delivers, fileScope (exact repo paths or narrow globs it may change, no overlap between tasks), 1-2 exemplar files to imitate, plannedLoc, approach (short instructions for the implementer).\n- Build in working slices, not layers. A criterion checked through the API or a screen passes only once that route or screen is wired, so the first task that adds a route or screen also wires the app's entry (the startup file, the router, the layout), and each later task adds its logic together with its own endpoint or screen. Code a slice needs (an entity, a helper) goes into the first slice that uses it, not into a task of its own. File scopes may not overlap, so have that first task register routes by convention (file-based routes, or every class of one kind found at startup): later tasks then add only their own files.\n- List a requirement on the task after which its criteria can pass. Do not leave the wiring to a last task that then holds every criterion checked through the app: each earlier task is then checked only there, in the longest and dearest task of the run. Only where the repo's structure forces layers, give the wiring task a dependsOn on every layer it serves.\n- dependsOn: only the tasks whose code this task really needs.\n- complexity, on every task: standard | rules-or-algorithm | external-dependency | compliance-sensitive | real-time | new-to-stack. Most tasks are standard. rules-or-algorithm is a task whose work is mostly business rules, validations, allowed state changes or calculations.\n- Test projects, test files and CI config are not in any file scope: tests are written separately.\n- stubs: for every NEW public type/method/endpoint the tests will call, give a compilable stub file (full file content) whose bodies throw new Error(\"not implemented\"), so tests compile before implementation. The stubs are built as they are, on the untouched code, before anything is implemented: a stub may only use types the repo already has or that another stub declares (an interface the endpoint classes share is a stub of its own). Existing APIs need no stubs. Stub paths must be inside a task's fileScope.\n- Outside systems (an email or SMS provider, a payment service, another company's API, a file store): the code reaches each one through one seam, a module of its own (one exported function per call it makes), and nothing else in the app calls the system directly. Use the seam the repo already has for that system; for a new one, give the seam as a stub, so the tests can put a stand-in behind it before the code exists. The system's settings (host, port, sender address, key) are read from configuration by name: put .env.example in the fileScope of the task that writes the seam's real implementation, and say in its approach that each new setting is declared there with an empty value.\n- protectedPathsDeclared: list any migration, CI, build-config or package-feed file you must change (a human will see it).\n- newDependencies: any npm package to add (name, version, registry). Prefer none.\n\nAPI CONTRACT. This product has a web app and an API in two repos, and both are built against one contract.\n- Give one more stub: path \"contracts/openapi.yaml\", content the full OpenAPI 3.0.3 document (YAML) of every operation the approved screens and requirements need. It is outside every task's fileScope: nobody implements it, both sides follow it.\n- Write it in block style, one key per line with two-space indents: no {...} or [...] on a line except an empty value (\"security: []\", \"{}\"). Put any text with an apostrophe, a colon or a # in double quotes. A file with whole operations on one line stops parsing at the first stray quote.\n- No YAML anchors, aliases or merge keys (&name, *name, <<): what is used twice is a schema, a response or a parameter under components, pointed at with $ref.\n- Every operation has an operationId (camelCase), its request body where it takes one, and every status code it answers with.\n- Schemas go under components/schemas with \"required\" lists. Take the field names and types from the approved screens' sample data; an id is an integer.\n- Every JSON response carries an \"example\" with believable data (the approved screens' sample rows): the web app's tests run against these examples.\n- The web app calls the API only through the client generated from this file (lib/api); do not plan a hand-written client, and put no path under lib/api in a stub or in a task's fileScope: the factory generates that folder.\n- What the spec lists as not changing with \"(API-SIDE)\" is behaviour the API side builds and tests: no task here builds it, and the contract still gives its operations and every status code they answer with.\n\nThe approved design is a new look: its implementers get design tokens (colours, type, corners, spacing as CSS variables). Put the app's global stylesheet or theme file in the fileScope of the first task that builds a screen, so the tokens are added once and the other screens use them.\n\nThe approved design is built in next-shadcn: before any task starts, the factory writes the component kit, the theme, the frame and navigation, and every approved page (its blocks, states, layers and text, with the approved sample data) into the repo. Those generated files are not in any task's scope.\n- TASK-1 is the design-system task: its fileScope is exactly the ui-scaffold's designSystemTask.fileScope (plus nothing else UI), and its approach is the designSystemTask.todo list.\n- Then one task per screen in ui-scaffold.screens: its fileScope includes that screen's container (and the API or service files the behaviour needs). The task gives the page real data, API calls, validation and the behaviour the requirements ask for, in the container; it does not restyle or rebuild the page.\n\nRepository map:\n","tools":["read_file","search","repo_map"],"user":"<artifact id=\"spec\" kind=\"spec\" sha=\"\">\n{\n \"assumptions\": [\n  \"Procurement, receiving, and demo data are supplied mock/source records; no live external connector is required.\",\n  \"Import outcomes are presented per row; unmatched records remain pending and unavailable to pen operations; over-capacity mappings are refused and displayed; repeated source identifiers are reported as duplicates.\",\n  \"Forecasts use current measured ADG when available and otherwise a configured default; target weight and gain-rate assumptions are editable per lot.\",\n  \"A wastage ratio affects consumption estimates and projections; actual wastage is a separate stock movement only when entered.\",\n  \"Feed conversion uses as-fed pounds; feed cost per pound of gain is not full ROI or profitability.\",\n  \"Ration optimization compares recorded cost and nutrition values against user-specified targets or constraints; users select a suggestion before use.\",\n  \"Feeding activities may be unplanned, may link to an existing plan, and require separate quantities for each affected lot in a multi-lot pen.\",\n  \"Lot total gain uses average-weight change and headcount applicable at the measurement dates.\",\n  \"The report date range selects the latest weight reading in range and feeding events up to that reading.\",\n  \"CSV export is included; drafts differ on whether PDF export is also required, so both requirements are retained separately.\",\n  \"Demo data loads on demand as a separate dataset, and added, already-present, and failed records are reported separately; partial loads are not labeled complete.\",\n  \"All users have the same access to product areas and operational controls.\",\n  \"Prompt history is omitted from the project and demo area.\",\n  \"Acceptance criteria verify responsive web-app screens and interactions using outcomes returned by a separate API service.\"\n ],\n \"critic\": [],\n \"lint\": [\n  {\n 